15) There: When there are no partitions—but if there are partitions, even a single partition to the west suffices, for the <i>Shechinah</i> is west (Beit Yosef); Levush <i>Orach Chayim</i> s. 3; <i>Sheyarei Knesset Hagedolah</i> s. 1—he should turn his back to the west. <i>Yeshu’ot Yaakov</i> there <i>Even HaEzer</i> s. 5, Raavad s. 8; some permit even if the partition is on the east when he turns his back east (Magen Avraham, Raavad there). <i>Tosafos Shabbat</i> “<i>Keitzad</i>” “<i>S. Plaim</i>”; it also fits <i>Kitzur Shulchan Aruch</i> siman 4 s. 2—the partition mentioned is not precise but conventional, one partition also suffices, <i>Tosafos</i> there. But Perisha/Magen and <i>Yeshu’ot Yaakov</i> there forbid if the partition is on the east—and their view warrants concern. See <i>Nishmat Adam</i> s. 5; partition height—Rav Ben Ohev Chayim there s. 9: it need not be ten handbreadths high provided the exposure is not visible, so if short he relieves right next to it so it shields him. Some say even with partitions avoid east-west <i>lechatchila</i>; it is proper to heed them where possible. Where one can build the privy initially, do not place it east-west but north-south (Raavad s. 7, Ben Ohev Chayim there); see Perisha/Magen s. 5. Some are careful not to build a privy except north-south.
